    {
      "id": "basilisk",
      "title": "The Basilisk",
      "short": "A small winged serpent, almost hidden at the centre of the picture.",
      "extended": "The creature is not named in the Met’s record; reading it as a basilisk is an interpretation. If it is one, it points to Psalm 91, which Satan quotes to Christ during the temptation. The next verse in the Latin Bible of Moretto’s day reads, in an early English translation: “Thou shalt walk upon the asp and the basilisk: and thou shalt trample under foot the lion and the dragon.”",
      "references": [
        "Psalm 91:11–13 (Vulgate 90:11–13)",
        "Matthew 4:6"
      ],
      "evidence": "plausible",
